  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe current construction achieves a C value of 0.380861. To find an improved upper bound, we will use a multi-resolution optimization approach. The key idea is to explore different problem sizes (n) with varying ad hoc manipulations and interpolation-based transfers, alongside novel regularization strategies and constrained optimization methods. The central approach involves iterating over a carefully selected set of n values, using each as a parent for localized multi-stage optimization. This strategy considers that the ergodic minimum may exist at boundaries between discrete solution spaces rather than solely within a single size. The method deliberately avoids over-parameterization to maintain time efficiency within the 1100-second budget.\n\n2. Mathematical specifications:\n- Primary optimization formulation: \n  minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \n  subject to: \u2211h_i*dx = 1, 0<=h_i<=1\n  where dx=2.0/n, and correlation mode='full'\n\n- Problem representation:\n  n: number of points (varied from 600 to 1000 in 100-point increments)\n  h: 1D numpy array of length n with values between 0 and 1\n  Constraint enforcement: Projective clipping with weighted redistribution to maintain integral 1/2\n\n- Optimization stages:\n  A. Initial configuration: Transfer parent vector using linear interpolation from fixed seeds\n  B. Convex Relaxation Phase: Formulate and solve for E infinity (Lawrence) constraint\n  C. Robust Local Search: Apply modified simplex optimizer with adjusted cooling schedule\n  D. Subgradient-Perturbation phase: Add controlled noise to escape local optima\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   Stage 1: Configuration management\n   a. Input parameters: n=800, \u03b1_lba=500, \u03b5_greedy=0.05\n   b. Initialize h using linear interpolation from fixed seeds\n   c. Project to feasible space ensuring \u2211h_i*dx=1 and bounded values\n\n   Stage 2: Convex Relaxation Cascade\n   a. Three-phase optimization:\n      Phase 1: NOA-splitting with adaptive damping\n      Phase 2: Barrier method with entropy regularization\n      Phase 3: Log barrier method with inexact steps\n\n   Stage 3: Localized descent\n   a. Simplex optimizer with constraint handling:\n      - Probability-based perturbation\n      - Dynamic parameter scaling\n      - Gradient-free movement in simplex space\n\n   Stage 4: Final perturbation and evaluation\n   a. Add carefully calibrated noise with NSPTO-based probability\n   b. Limit iterations based on time budget\n   c. Re-evaluate offspring candidates with same dataset methodology\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   Default parameters:\n   - Resize factor = 100\n   - Noise scale = 0.01-0.05\n   - Initial damping = 0.2\n   - Cooling rate = 0.95\n   Variations triggered:\n   - When all minimizer values are too similar (<0.005 difference)\n   - Three times no improvement detected\n   - Project failure to previous candidate\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   Time allocation:\n   - Initial configuration: 5 seconds\n   - Optimization phases: 15% \n   - Main search: 40%\n   - Cool down: 10%\n   - Final evaluation: 10%\n   Validation checks:\n   - Maintain h[i] \u2208 [0,1] by projection\n   - Scale-by-latitude error handling \n   - Immediate fallback to parent solution if NaN detected\n\n6. Return contract:\n   - Return (h_values, final_c5, n)\n   - final_c5 = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))*dx\n   - Check: 0<=h_values[i]<=1 for all i\n   - Check: sum(h_values)*dx \u2248 1 (within \u03b5=1e-7)\n   - Compute with same codebase method\n   - Maintain n as parent or transfer size\n\nFor all n values, use adaptive optimization with three doubling iterations for each stage. For each optimization step, solve the problem in fixed-length increments. When shifting from n_low to n_high, linearly interpolate and adjust constraint enforcement. Monitor L1 stability and halve algorithm parameters if computational instability detected. Set maximum n=1000 due to time constraints.\n\n<NoCodeUntilDeadlineStrategy>",
